This tells us:

✔ Container started successfully
✔ Container allocated more memory than allowed
✔ Kernel OOM-killed the process
✔ Kubernetes restarted it
✔ Backoff kicked in → CrashLoopBackOff

🧠 Why the pod shows Running but container is crashing

You might notice:

Status: Running
State:  Waiting (CrashLoopBackOff)

This is normal and important.

Pod = Running (object exists, scheduled)
Container = failing repeatedly
Kubernetes is retrying with exponential backoff

👉 Many production dashboards show “Running” even during outages — this is why logs & events matter.
-------------------------------------------------------------------

🔴 What actually went wrong (root cause)
Key error:
exec: "--vm": executable file not found in $PATH

Why this happened

You used this container spec:

image: polinux/stress
args: ["--vm", "1", "--vm-bytes", "500M"]


But BusyBox-style images don’t have an entrypoint that accepts args like this automatically.
The container runtime tried to run:
"--vm"
as a command — which obviously doesn’t exist.

👉 This is NOT a Kubernetes memory issue
👉 This is a container command/args misconfiguration

That’s why you see:
Reason: StartError
Exit code 128
No OOMKill


🧠 Senior Mental Model (important)

There are two very different failure classes:

Failure Type	Where it fails	Example
StartError	Container runtime	Bad command / args
OOMKilled	Kernel	Memory limit exceeded

Your failure is StartError, not OOM.

“The pod entered CrashLoopBackOff because the container exceeded its memory limit and was OOMKilled. Kubernetes restarted it automatically, and backoff increased with each failure.”

Compare CPU vs Memory Enforcement (Think Like SRE)
--------------------------------------------------------------
Ask yourself:

CPU limit → throttling (soft)
Memory limit → OOMKill (hard)
Which is more dangerous? (memory)

This is why memory limits are critical.
